{{Life in Balisca}}

In 2025, [[W:Islam|Islam]] was determined to be the second-largest [[W:Religion|religious group]] in Balisca, after [[Christianity in Balisca|Christianity]].

Islam has been a major religion in the [[Baliscan archipelago]], beginning with the [[Crown of Aragon|Aragonese exploration of the region]] and expanding significantly following [[W:Forced conversions of Muslims in Spain|its prohibition by the modern Spanish state]] in the mid-16th century and the [[W:Expulsion of the Moriscos|expulsion of the Moriscos]] in the early 17th century, resulting in the migration of around 500,000 people, to include thousands of [[W:New Christians|New Christians]] (including [[W:Crypto-Muslims|Crypto-Muslims]] and [[W:Crypto-Jews|Crypto-Jews]]). Despite a significant proportion of the Moriscos returning to Castile, or evading expulsion by resettling in Aragon (specifically the [[W:Kingdom of Valencia|Kingdom of Valencia]]), the center of Spanish Islamic culture would largely shift to the [[Viceroyalty of New Aragon|Baliscan colonies]] by the dawn of the 18th century. Significantly larger numbers of enslaved [[W:Islam in Africa|African Muslims]] were brought to the Baliscan islands following the beginning of the {{W|Atlantic slave trade}}. Historically, an estimated 60% of enslaved people brought to Balisca from {{W|West Africa|West}}/{{W|Central Africa}} were Muslims.

In the [[2025 Baliscan census]], it was determined that 11.2% of the population of Balisca identified as Muslim (this does not include [[W:Cultural Muslims|Cultural Muslims]], individuals that are not religious and do not practice the faith). Balisca's Muslim population has continued to grow since the second half of the 20th century, with increased immigration from former European colonies and protectorates in [[W:Africa|Africa]] and the [[W:Middle East|Middle East]]. This recent growth has been buoyed by sustained arrivals from countries such as [[W:Senegal|Senegal]] and [[W:Nigeria|Nigeria]] in [[W:West Africa|West Africa]], [[W:Pakistan|Pakistan]] in South Asia, and a continuous flow of predominately [[W:Sunni Islam|Sunni]] [[Arab Baliscans|Arab]] migrants from the [[W:Levant|Levant]].

